Category 12,  Topic 2
Message 3         Sat Sep 05, 1987
W.ASKEY                      at 13:23 PDT
          
Willie -- The definition of a "best Forth" is a little tricky, but given your
guidelines (most similar to "STARTING FORTH" and "THINKING FORTH"), I suppose
I'd suggest MasterFORTH by Micromotion (1-213-821-4340). I've never used
MasterFORTH, but it is supposed to be "exactly" FORTH-83 standard and thus
should be very similar (on a high- language level) to Brodie's books (I'm
assuming that you are using the second edition of "STARTING FORTH," the one
which uses FORTH-83).  However (now for some editorializing), if you plan on
using Forth on the Macintosh then I think you should consider going to a 32-
bit  Forth such as MACH2 or MacFORTH Plus.  Of these two products, MACH2 would
be closest to Brodie's books (MACH2 is nearly FORTH-83 standard, except that
it uses 32-bit wide stacks, variables, and operators). MACH2 also has a
product support roundtable on GEnie, so maybe you can browse there for some
question and answers (you can go to the MACH RT with a "MOVE 450").  Also, see
my quick "review" of MacFORTH Plus and MACH2 in Category 4. -- Waymen P.S. I
agree with Dennis' comments about debating "best" products, I  suggest that
you investigate each product and make a decision based upon what YOU like
best.
