Category 12,  Topic 2
Message 4         Sat Sep 05, 1987
LMORGENSTERN                 at 16:08 PDT
          
 I have MicroMotion Forth. Its kernel is almost the same as F83. It has a lot
of important enhancements (some are extra cost):
 More powerful disk access, using DOS handles.
 Access to Ascii text files
 A neat editor
 Headerless code without recompiling
 Transient/resident (to get rid of words used only during 
  compilation--including the assembler)
 Overlays
 Floating point and 8087 support.
 Etc. Several important points need to be made. While you are learning Forth
stick with F83. It is very powerful, and has a lot of really amazing
----------
Category 12,  Topic 2
Message 5         Sat Sep 05, 1987
LMORGENSTERN                 at 16:11 PDT
          
continued features, multitasking and trace debugging being only two. Its only
real weaknesses result from the desire of the authors (Laxen and Perry) to
make the system compatible with both CP/M and DOS. When you start to write
applications, you will need something more, and MicroMotion is a good step. I
don't know how it compares with other commercial Forths. If you get into
professional work, a good programming environment is essential, and you should
investigate carefully before you buy.
