Category 12,  Topic 2
Message 6         Tue Sep 08, 1987
P.COOPER                     at 20:19 PDT
          
Willie, while I agree with Waymen's comments about taking sides with respect
to MACH2 and MacFORTH, one main concern when programming the Mac is the
toolbox interface.  MACH2 implements all calls EXACTLY as described in Inside
Macintosh (essential reading), whereas MacFORTH has its own high level
routines which don't always use the same parameters.  MACH2's approach makes
it easy to translate routines from other languages into FORTH (many are
published in MacTutor, an excellent mag), and many of the books on the Mac
will probably make more sense as most other languages on the Mac follow the IM
interfaces too.  MACH2's assembler is also more like a standard 68000
assembler.  Hope this makes your decision easier--or you could buy both!
                                       Peter
