Category 12,  Topic 2
Message 7         Tue Sep 08, 1987
W.ASKEY                      at 22:03 PDT
          
John -- I think you would agree that MacFORTH Plus is really not FORTH-79 or 
FORTH-83. I believe, however, that someone just beginning to learn Forth would
find more FORTH-79 gotcha's than he or she might like. The list of words in
MacFORTH Plus which are FORTH-79-like (and  different in FORTH-83) is fairly
significant, some which come to  mind are -- PICK, ROLL, FIND, WORD, LEAVE,
.", ' (TICK), and the round to zero rather than round to negative infinity. 
It's   true that MacFORTH has elements of FORTH-83 within it, but the 
"dangerous" words are the ones which changed function between the   FORTH-79
and FORTH-83 standards.  Please do not take this as a criticism of MacFORTH,
MacFORTH is a real "power" language which  has many, many useful extensions. 
However, if someone asked me  to categorize MacFORTH, I think I would be
conservative (safe) by saying that it is FORTH-79 with many additions (some of
which are from the FORTH-83 standard).

Also (from a previous message), for newcomers it should be made clear that F83
is NOT the same as the FORTH-83 standard.

I've downloaded the F83 source, deARC'd it (successfully), and fixed  some
problems with the format of the source text (line formating lost   -- no CR's -
- and control characters in the source text -- WORDSTAR?). Now I have two
questions for F83 users, has anyone ported F83 to the Macintosh, and is that
port available in the public domain?      -- Waymen

P.S. I wrote a program to reformat and zap control characters from the RT
library source for F83, I'll upload that application for Macintosh  users who
may have had similar problems with other RT downloads.
